# iOS port spec — 116 markdown task files (00-CONTEXT … 90-STATE) plus the agent manual.
# The whole folder, root-anchored: it holds nothing but .md, so this is exactly the spec
# and it also covers files added later. These WERE tracked (11 `docs(ios-spec):` commits
# are on origin/main), so `git rm -r --cached ios-spec` is what actually stops the
# tracking — the ignore alone does nothing to files git already knows about.
/ios-spec/

# Secrets, signing & environment (never commit)
# Environment files
.env
.env.*
*.env
# Signing keystores & configs
*.jks
*.keystore
keystore.properties
signing.properties
*.p12
*.pfx
# Certificates & private keys
*.pem
*.cer
*.der
*.key
# Apple private keys (PKCS#8 PEM, EC P-256), both named AuthKey_<KEYID>.p8:
# the APNs auth key (→ Firebase) and the App Store Connect API key (→ xcrun altool).
# Each downloads exactly once. *.pem and *.key do NOT catch these — gitignore globs the
# filename, not the contents. Note this also hides them from `git status`, so the leak
# check must read the index instead: git ls-files | grep -E '\.p8$'
*.p8
# Service-account / credential JSON & secret properties
*-service-account*.json
*service_account*.json
*-credentials.json
credentials.json
secrets.properties
secrets.xml

